A quick search using PNW native milkweed turned up two varieties.
This is one;
“Showy Milkweed (Asclepias speciosa) is a perennial plant native to western North America, known for its showy, pinkish-purple flowers and its role as a host plant for Monarch butterflies. It's a popular choice for native plant gardens and restoration projects due to its ability to support pollinators and improve soil health. While it spreads moderately through rhizomes, it's generally considered less aggressive than some other milkweed species, like Common Milkweed.”
Consider if you have grazing pasture lands near it may not be a good thing to plant. The PNW variety do not spread as fast or aggressively as some other milkweed varieties, but in general milkweed has some impact on agriculture in that it is considered toxic to livestock(all livestock or only certain grazing stock it did not specify) What the search reports;
 “Toxicity: Like other milkweeds, it contains cardenolides in its sap, which can be toxic to livestock if ingested in large quantities. “
Maybe plant in a container flower bed.
